original_raw_output:
Here's an example showing improper path traversal validation versus proper validation:

```java
// Vulnerable code: allows path traversal attacks
public class FileDownloader {
    public void downloadFile(String fileName, HttpServletResponse response) {
        try {
            // VULNERABLE: Direct use of user input without proper validation
            File file = new File("/app/files/" + fileName);

            FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream(file);
            BufferedInputStream bis = new BufferedInputStream(fis);

            response.setContentType("application/octet-stream");
            OutputStream os = response.getOutputStream();

            byte[] buffer = new byte[1024];
            int bytesRead;
            while ((bytesRead = bis.read(buffer)) != -1) {
                os.write(buffer, 0, bytesRead);
            }

            bis.close();
            fis.close();
            os.close();
        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```
***
```java
// Secure code: implements proper path validation
public class FileDownloader {
    public void downloadFile(String fileName, HttpServletResponse response) {
        try {
            // Normalize the path and validate against directory traversal
            Path requestedFile = Paths.get(fileName).normalize();
            if (requestedFile.startsWith("..")) {
                throw new SecurityException("Path traversal attempt detected");
            }

            // Use Path API for secure file handling
            Path basePath = Paths.get("/app/files");
            Path filePath = basePath.resolve(requestedFile).normalize();

            // Verify the resolved path is still within the base directory
            if (!filePath.startsWith(basePath)) {
                throw new SecurityException("Access denied");
            }

            // Safe to proceed with file operations
            Files.copy(filePath, response.getOutputStream());
            response.setContentType("application/octet-stream");

        } catch (IOException e) {
            response.setStatus(HttpServletResponse.SC_INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR);
        } catch (SecurityException e) {
            response.setStatus(HttpServletResponse.SC_FORBIDDEN);
        }
    }
}
```

raw_output:
Here's an example showing improper vs. proper use of Java's String.split() method, which could lead to a regex DoS vulnerability:

```java
public class UserInputProcessor {
    public boolean validateInput(String userInput) {
        // vulnerable: uncontrolled input in split() can cause regex DoS
        String[] parts = userInput.split(userInput);

        // process the parts
        for (String part : parts) {
            if (part.length() > 1000) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        UserInputProcessor processor = new UserInputProcessor();
        // Malicious input like "(a+)+" could cause exponential processing time
        processor.validateInput("(a+)+");
    }
}
```
***
```java
public class UserInputProcessor {
    public boolean validateInput(String userInput) {
        // Safe: Using literal string delimiter instead of user input
        // Also adding input validation and length checks
        if (userInput == null || userInput.length() > 1000) {
            return false;
        }

        String[] parts = userInput.split(",");

        // Additional safety: limiting the number of splits
        if (parts.length > 100) {
            return false;
        }

        // process the parts
        for (String part : parts) {
            if (part.length() > 1000) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        UserInputProcessor processor = new UserInputProcessor();
        processor.validateInput("safe,input,here");
    }
}
```

